 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 503057,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/33fd27e5ea5176e6.png\" alt=\"A table with seven columns and seven rows summarizes the results of seven experiments that examined the locations of binding sites for sodium ions, potassium ions, and A T P. From left to right, the headings in the columns are as follows: Experiment; A T P present inside cells?; A T P present outside cells?; Ouabain present inside cells?; Oubain present outside cells?; Was N a plus transported?; Was K plus transported? From top to bottom, the Experiment column contains rows numbered 1 through 7. The data in the rows is as follows. A T P was present inside cells in experiments 1, 2, 5, 6, and 7 and was not present inside cells in experiments 3 and 4. A T P was present outside cells in experiments 1 and 3 only. Ouabain was present inside cells in experiments 5 and 6 only. Ouabain was present outside cells in experiments 5 and 7 only. N a plus was transported in experiments 1, 2, and 7 only. K plus was transported in experiments 1, 2, and 6 only.\"></p>\n<p>The sodium-potassium <span data-math=\"(\\text{Na}^{+}/\\text{K}^{+})\">(\\text{Na}^{+}/\\text{K}^{+})</span> pump functions like an anti-porter transporting <span data-math=\"\\text{Na}^{+} \\!\">\\text{Na}^{+} \\!</span> and <span data-math=\"\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}\">\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}</span> across membranes using ATP. This protein spans the membrane with intracellular and extracellular domains. It has a binding site for <span data-math=\"\\text{Na}^{+} {\\!}\">\\text{Na}^{+} {\\!}</span>, <span data-math=\"\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}\">\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}</span>, and ATP. An experiment was conducted to determine the locations of these binding sites. Artificial cells were created and incubated in buffers containing ATP, ouabain (or oubain), <span data-math=\"\\text{Na}^{+} {\\!}\">\\text{Na}^{+} {\\!}</span>, and <span data-math=\"\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}\">\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}</span> in varying combinations inside and outside of the cell as indicated in the chart. The transport of <span data-math=\"\\text{Na}^{+} {\\!}\">\\text{Na}^{+} {\\!}</span> and <span data-math=\"\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}\">\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}</span> was measured to determine activity of the <span data-math=\"\\text{Na}^{+}/\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}\">\\text{Na}^{+}/\\text{K}^{+} {\\!}</span> pump. Which of the following conclusions is supported by the data?</p>\n",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 988359,
     "content_html": "Ouabain does not disrupt ATP binding to the <span data-math=\"\\text{Na}^+/\\text{K}^+\\!\">\\text{Na}^+/\\text{K}^+\\!</span> pump."
    },
    {
     "id": 988360,
     "content_html": "ATP is required for transport of <span data-math=\"\\text{Na}^+\\!\">\\text{Na}^+\\!</span> and not for transport of <span data-math=\"\\text{K}^+\\!\">\\text{K}^+\\!</span>."
    },
    {
     "id": 988361,
     "content_html": "The ATP binding site of the <span data-math=\"\\text{Na}^+/\\text{K}^+\\!\">\\text{Na}^+/\\text{K}^+\\!</span> pump is located on the intracellular domain of the pump."
    },
    {
     "id": 988362,
     "content_html": "The ATP binding site of the <span data-math=\"\\text{Na}^+/\\text{K}^+\\!\">\\text{Na}^+/\\text{K}^+\\!</span> pump is located on the extracellular domain of the pump."
    }
   ],
